      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "⏰ SABAH OKUMASIYLA DEVAM EDELİM ⏰\n\nIn the abyssal zone of the ocean, where sunlight fails to penetrate, organisms have evolved fascinating adaptations to survive in absolute darkness. Chief among these is bioluminescence—the production and emission of light by a living organism. Unlike terrestrial creatures, which rarely possess this ability, a vast majority of deep-sea species rely on chemical reactions to generate illumination. This biological phenomenon serves a multitude of purposes crucial for survival in an otherwise inhospitable environment.\n\nFor instance, the anglerfish utilizes a glowing lure suspended above its mouth to attract unsuspecting prey. Conversely, other species use bioluminescence as a defense mechanism to startle predators or camouflage themselves against faint downwelling light, a process known as counterillumination. Furthermore, light signals play a vital role in species recognition and mate selection. By studying the enzymes responsible for these reactions, biotechnologists are developing innovative tools for medical imaging, demonstrating that secrets from the ocean floor can illuminate advancements in human medicine.",
